{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-opg-827-coloring-random-subgraphs-875e63b3-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T08:00:00Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-opg-827-coloring-random-subgraphs-875e63b3-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-opg-827-coloring-random-subgraphs-875e63b3-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-opg-827-coloring-random-subgraphs-875e63b3-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/OPG-827"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "If $G$ is a graph and $p \\in [0,1]$, we let $G_p$ denote a subgraph of $G$ where each edge of $G$ appears in $G_p$ with independently with probability $p$. Problem Does there exist a constant $c$ so that ${\\mathbb E}(\\chi(G_{1/2})) > c \\frac{\\chi(G)}{\\log \\chi(G)}$?"}, "statement_sha256": "30c6af29906319d844b615d2ebf68da93934531294fb853af66c497c2acffb05"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-opg-827-coloring-random-subgraphs-875e63b3-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/OPG-827"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256": "65f0b3d574bd6d837403149dab6434c0f571709f7eb5bb066c7e8774bad13f34"}], "problem_contract_sha256": "d68958a217a78183da76244394d69010c19c1601b1986b23f8f897bd3c57f8d5", "problem_id": "problem:um-opg-827-coloring-random-subgraphs-875e63b3", "root_obli